81":"reproach","H4325":"water","H6013":"deep","H1697":"word","H6310":"lip","H376":"man","H5158":"river","H5042":"to bubble","H4726":"fountain","H2451":"wisdom","H5375":"raise","H6440":"before","H2896":"pleasant","H5186":"to stretch","H6662":"righteous","H4941":"judgement","H8193":"lips","H7379":"strife","H4112":"blow","H7121":"call to","H4288":"terror","H4170":"snare","H5315":"soul","H5372":"whisperer","H3859":"to swallow","H1992":"they(masc.)","H3381":"to go down","H2315":"chamber","H990":"abdomen","H7503":"to slacken","H4399":"work","H251":"male-sibling","H1931":"he/she/it","H1167":"master","H7843":"to ruin","H4026":"(Hananel) Tower","H5797":"strength","H8034":"name","H3068":"LORD","H7323":"run","H7682":"to exalt","H1952":"substance","H6223":"rich","H7151":"town","H2346":"wall","H4906":"figure","H7667":"breaking","H1361":"to exult","H3519":"glory","H6038":"humility","H7725":"return","H2962":"before","H8085":"hear","H200":"folly","H3639":"shame","H7307":"spirit","H3557":"to sustain","H4245":"sickness","H5218":"stricken","H4310":"who?","H995":"to understand","H7069":"to buy","H1847":"knowledge","H241":"ear","H2450":"wise","H4976":"gift","H120":"man","H7337":"to enlarge","H1419":"large","H5148":"to lead","H7223":"first","H7453":"neighbor","H2713":"to search","H4079":"contention","H7673":"to cease","H1486":"allotted","H996":"between","H6099":"mighty","H6586":"to transgress","H4066":"strife","H1280":"bar","H759":"palace","H6529":"fruit","H7646":"to satisfy","H8393":"produce","H4194":"death","H2416":"alive","H3027":"hand","H3956":"tongue","H157":"lover","H398":"to eat","H4672":"to find","H802":"woman","H6329":"to promote","H7522":"acceptance","H8469":"supplication","H1696":"speak","H7326":"be poor","H6030":"to dwell","H5794":"strong","H7489":"be evil","H3426":"there","H1695":"cleaving"},"ccc":{"5":[2476]},"cccp":{"2476":"__P8K#"},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Reproach. True friendship resembles charity, and bears all things, 1 Corinthians xiii. 4. Hebrew now reads Thave, “desire;” instead of Thuane, occasion, or “pretext,” which must have been in the copies of the Septuagint and Vulgate. (Calmet) — Protestants, “through desire, a man having separated himself, seeketh and intermeddleth with all wisdom.” The solitary seeks heaven. (Haydock)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Heart. Conformable to his passions. (Calmet) — Hebrew, “unless to lay open his heart.” He wishes to appear wise, and to justify his wicked designs. (Haydock)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Contemneth both God and man, Luke xviii. 4. Hebrew, “is contemned” in his turn. (Calmet) — He is like a man abandoned by the physician. (Op. Imp. in Matt. Hom. 40.)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Man, who is just and wise. His advice deserves attention, chap. xx. 5."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Quarrels. Hebrew, “blows.” Septuagint, “death;” (ver. 7.) which are the usual consequences of quarrels."],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Tongued. Hebrew, “calumniator.” He pretends to wish well to those of whom he speaks, or else to guard the company against deceit. (Calmet) — “If the devil be upon the detractor’s tongue, he is in the ears of those who pay attention to him.” (St. Francis de Sales) — Fear, &c., is in the Septuagint, above. The Vulgate retains both this and the new version of St. Jerome."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Brother. Like him, as both end in poverty, chap. x. 4., and xii. 11."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Name. Essence, or protection of God. The rich depends on his own wealth."],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Heareth the end of the question, chap. i. 5."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Infirmity of the flesh, Matthew xxvi. 41. — That is. Theodotion, “is wounded, who shall support?”"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Princes. He easily finds access by showing submission. (Calmet)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Him. To see that he act with sincerity."],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Lot. Chap. xvi. 33. Septuagint, “silence.” (Calmet) — But Grabe substitutes “lot.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Judgments of many are more deserving of credit. Hebrew, “a brother offended, is like a strong place, and their quarrels,” &c. They are not easily reconciled. Civil wars are most terrible. (Calmet)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Satisfied. Those who are guarded in their words get employment. (Haydock) (Chap. xii. 14.)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Love it, and speak well or ill, shall receive accordingly, Matthew xii. 37."],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Good wife. Good is not in Hebrew, but should be understood, as it is expressed in the Complutensian (Calmet) and Alexandrian Septuagint. (Haydock) — He that, &c., occurs not in Hebrew, Sixtus V, &c. But it is found in Septuagint and Arabic. The Syriac omits the last sentence. — Wicked. St. Augustine had frequently asserted that a divorce was only of counsel: but this he retracted, when he reflected on this text. (Retractions i. 19.) — The Hebrews, Athenians, and Romans, followed the same practice with adulteresses. (Selden, Ux. iii. 16.; Dem. in Neæram, &c.) — Hermas (past. i. 4.) prescribes that the penitent shall be received again, but not often. In case of divorce, the fathers still permit not a second marriage, that the parties may be reconciled. They enjoin the husband to put away only such as are incorrigible. (St. Augustine, Adul. ii. 3.) (Calmet)"],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Brother. The ties of nature are not so strong as those of friendship. (Calmet) — Hebrew, “a man that hath friends must shew himself friendly; and there is a friend that sticketh closer than a brother.” (Protestants) (Haydock) — Ut ameris ama. (Martial)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}